import Mathlib.Tactic
import Proofs.QMClosure.ArityBoundsInterferenceOrder
import Proofs.QMClosure.SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration

namespace IDT
namespace QMClosure

/-!
# Composition and the binary arity of readout weights

Two results and one research hypothesis, connecting the grade equivalence of
`SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration` to the kernel language of
`ArityBoundsInterferenceOrder` and to composition.

First, the kernel bridge. On three alternatives, a weight generated by a
kernel on pairs in the sense of `kernelWeight` is exactly a pairwise
generated weight in the sense of `TwoDecomposable`, and the two equivalences
compose: a weight with vanishing Sorkin third-order term on all disjoint
triples is generated by SOME kernel on pairs
(`vanishing_third_order_is_kernel_generation`). Together with
`SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration` this discharges the open obligation
`proveTheConverseFromVanishingThirdOrderToBinaryGeneration` of
`ArityBoundsInterferenceOrder`: vanishing third-order interference forces
binary generation; the two are one property.

Second, the escape witness. The pointwise product of two pairwise generated
weights need NOT be pairwise generated: on four alternatives, the block
weight (1 when the selection contains {0, 1}, plus 1 when it contains {2, 3})
is pairwise generated, while its square is not -- its third-order term on the
split {2}, {3}, {0, 1} equals 2, nonzero. Products ADD grade: grade one
composed with grade one stays grade two (proved in
`SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration` as the Guo-Cao-Chen-Yin corollary), but
grade two composed with grade two escapes. So composing readouts by
multiplying weights is not the composite readout rule; a composite of two
second-order readouts cannot be second order through weight multiplication.
This is a finite-arithmetic companion, inside the weight calculus, of the
decoherence-functional literature: arbitrary grade-two measures do not
compose either. Strong positivity is maximal against adding systems compatible
with all strongly positive systems (Boes-Navascues 2017). The uniqueness theorem
of Dowker-Wilkes (2022), Theorem 3, assumes BOTH tensor closure and Galois
self-duality; tensor closure by itself does not select strong positivity.
CoherentReferencePositivity supplies a finite rational counterexample to the
weaker self-copy argument and states the signed-reference import explicitly.

The research hypothesis, NOT a theorem here: composition closure forces
binary arity (the C-arity conjecture of the July 2026 research notes). Every
known readout world with genuinely ternary structure fails composition:
density cubes have third-order interference and violate local tomography
(K(4) = 22 against 16, Dakic-Paterek-Brukner 2014); quartic quantum theory
has all orders and defective composites; Galley-Masanes 2018 show any
modification of the Born rule that changes the interference order violates
purification or local tomography; Barnum-Lee-Scandolo-Selby 2017 derive
at-most-second-order interference from purity principles including
purification. If composition closure forces pairwise generation, then the
route "composability -> binary arity -> grade two -> Born exponent" would
select the exponent without ever mentioning amplitudes, and the earlier
circularity verdict (the strong readings of "stable distinction is pairwise
data" are Born itself) would be bypassed: the selector would be a property of
composition, not of distinction. The hypothesis is deliberately NOT
formalised as a bag of abstract Prop flags -- the audit lesson of
`S2BornProofSearch` -- but stated here in prose with its literature anchors
and recorded as the open obligation below. The decisive experiment is the
two-context ternary composability probe: the H1 kill construction of section
174.80 tested a ternary witness in a SINGLE context; no one has tested
whether a ternary discipline survives a gluing with a second context under a
local-tomography demand. Literature predicts it cannot.

Scope. Three and four alternatives over ℚ; kernel weights, no complex
amplitudes, no positivity, no Hilbert carrier, no Born rule derived, no
primitive-base semantics. The literature numbers cited above are anchors,
not results of this repository.
-/

/-! ## The kernel bridge on three alternatives -/

theorem univ_offDiag_fin3 :
    (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin 3)).offDiag
      = insert ((0 : Fin 3), 1) (insert ((1 : Fin 3), 0)
          (insert ((0 : Fin 3), 2) (insert ((2 : Fin 3), 0)
            (insert ((1 : Fin 3), 2) (insert ((2 : Fin 3), 1) ∅))))) := by
  ext p
  fin_cases p <;> simp [Finset.mem_offDiag]

/--
On `0/1` selection flags the kernel weight of `ArityBoundsInterferenceOrder`
is exactly the singleton-plus-ordered-pair shape of `TwoDecomposable`, with
the diagonal of the kernel as the singleton part.
-/
theorem flag_bridge (k : Fin 3 → Fin 3 → ℚ) (S : Finset (Fin 3)) :
    (∑ i ∈ S, k i i) + ∑ p ∈ S.offDiag, k p.1 p.2
      = kernelWeight k (ind S 0) (ind S 1) (ind S 2) := by
  classical
  have e0 : ∑ i ∈ S, k i i = ∑ i ∈ S, ind S i * k i i :=
    Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun i hi => by simp [ind, hi])
  rw [e0,
    Finset.sum_subset (Finset.subset_univ S) (fun i _ hi => by simp [ind, hi]),
    offDiag_sum_ind S Finset.univ (Finset.subset_univ S) k,
    univ_offDiag_fin3, Fin.sum_univ_three]
  unfold kernelWeight ind
  by_cases h0 : (0 : Fin 3) ∈ S
  <;> by_cases h1 : (1 : Fin 3) ∈ S
  <;> by_cases h2 : (2 : Fin 3) ∈ S
  <;> simp [h0, h1, h2]
  <;> try ring

/-- Kernel generation is pairwise generation. -/
theorem kernel_generated_is_two_decomposable (k : Fin 3 → Fin 3 → ℚ) :
    TwoDecomposable (fun S : Finset (Fin 3) =>
      kernelWeight k (ind S 0) (ind S 1) (ind S 2)) :=
  ⟨fun i => k i i, k, fun S => (flag_bridge k S).symm⟩

/--
The converse in the kernel language, closing the loop with
`SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration`: a weight on three alternatives whose
Sorkin third-order term vanishes on all disjoint nonempty triples is
generated by some kernel on pairs. This is the formal content behind the
open obligation `proveTheConverseFromVanishingThirdOrderToBinaryGeneration`
of `ArityBoundsInterferenceOrder`.
-/
theorem vanishing_third_order_is_kernel_generation
    (mu : Finset (Fin 3) → ℚ) (hempty : mu ∅ = 0)
    (h3 : ∀ A B C : Finset (Fin 3), A.Nonempty → B.Nonempty → C.Nonempty →
      Disjoint A B → Disjoint A C → Disjoint B C → sorkinI3 mu A B C = 0) :
    ∃ k : Fin 3 → Fin 3 → ℚ,
      ∀ S : Finset (Fin 3),
        mu S = kernelWeight k (ind S 0) (ind S 1) (ind S 2) := by
  obtain ⟨a, b, hmu⟩ := vanishing_i3_is_two_decomposable mu hempty h3
  refine ⟨fun i j => if i = j then a i else b i j, fun S => ?_⟩
  have hdiag : ∀ i : Fin 3,
      (fun (x y : Fin 3) => if x = y then a x else b x y) i i = a i :=
    fun i => by simp
  have hoff : ∀ p ∈ S.offDiag,
      (fun (x y : Fin 3) => if x = y then a x else b x y) p.1 p.2 = b p.1 p.2 := by
    intro p hp
    simp [(Finset.mem_offDiag.mp hp).2.2]
  have hb := flag_bridge (fun (x y : Fin 3) => if x = y then a x else b x y) S
  rw [hmu S, ← hb, Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun i _ => hdiag i),
    Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun p hp => hoff p hp)]

/-! ## The escape witness on four alternatives -/

theorem twoDecomposable_add {β : Type} [DecidableEq β] (mu nu : Finset β → ℚ)
    (hmu : TwoDecomposable mu) (hnu : TwoDecomposable nu) :
    TwoDecomposable (fun S => mu S + nu S) := by
  obtain ⟨a1, b1, h1⟩ := hmu
  obtain ⟨a2, b2, h2⟩ := hnu
  refine ⟨fun i => a1 i + a2 i, fun i j => b1 i j + b2 i j, fun S => ?_⟩
  simp only []
  rw [h1 S, h2 S, Finset.sum_add_distrib, Finset.sum_add_distrib]
  ring

theorem sum_delta_eq_ind {β : Type} [DecidableEq β] (u : β) (S : Finset β) :
    ∑ i ∈ S, (if i = u then (1 : ℚ) else 0) = (if u ∈ S then (1 : ℚ) else 0) := by
  by_cases hu : u ∈ S
  · rw [if_pos hu,
      Finset.sum_eq_single u (fun b _ hb => by simp [hb])
        (fun hnu => absurd hu hnu)]
    simp
  · rw [if_neg hu]
    exact Finset.sum_eq_zero fun i hi => by
      by_cases h : i = u
      · exact absurd (h ▸ hi) hu
      · simp [h]

/--
A block weight: 1 when the selection contains {0, 1}, plus 1 when it
contains {2, 3}. Each block is a product of two additive (grade-one) delta
weights, so the block weight is pairwise generated by the product lemma of
`SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration`.
-/
def blockWeight (S : Finset (Fin 4)) : ℚ :=
  (if ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4)) ⊆ S then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
    + (if ({2, 3} : Finset (Fin 4)) ⊆ S then (1 : ℚ) else 0)

theorem blockWeight_eq (S : Finset (Fin 4)) :
    blockWeight S
      = (∑ i ∈ S, if i = (0 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
        * (∑ j ∈ S, if j = (1 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
      + (∑ i ∈ S, if i = (2 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
        * (∑ j ∈ S, if j = (3 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0) := by
  rw [sum_delta_eq_ind, sum_delta_eq_ind, sum_delta_eq_ind, sum_delta_eq_ind]
  unfold blockWeight
  simp only [Finset.insert_subset_iff, Finset.singleton_subset_iff]
  by_cases h0 : (0 : Fin 4) ∈ S <;> by_cases h1 : (1 : Fin 4) ∈ S <;>
    by_cases h2 : (2 : Fin 4) ∈ S <;> by_cases h3 : (3 : Fin 4) ∈ S <;>
    simp [h0, h1, h2, h3]

/-- The block weight is pairwise generated. -/
theorem blockWeight_two_decomposable : TwoDecomposable blockWeight := by
  have h1 := product_of_additive_is_two_decomposable
    (fun i => if i = (0 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
    (fun j => if j = (1 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
  have h2 := product_of_additive_is_two_decomposable
    (fun i => if i = (2 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
    (fun j => if j = (3 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
  have hfun : blockWeight = fun S : Finset (Fin 4) =>
      ((fun T : Finset (Fin 4) =>
        (∑ i ∈ T, if i = (0 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
          * (∑ j ∈ T, if j = (1 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)) S
      + (fun T : Finset (Fin 4) =>
        (∑ i ∈ T, if i = (2 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)
          * (∑ j ∈ T, if j = (3 : Fin 4) then (1 : ℚ) else 0)) S) :=
    funext blockWeight_eq
  rw [hfun]
  exact twoDecomposable_add _ _ h1 h2

/--
The escape, computed: the pointwise square of the block weight has
third-order interference 2 on the split {2}, {3}, {0, 1}.
-/
theorem block_weight_square_i3 :
    sorkinI3 (fun S => blockWeight S * blockWeight S)
      ({2} : Finset (Fin 4)) ({3} : Finset (Fin 4)) ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4)) = 2 := by
  have e1 : blockWeight (({2} : Finset (Fin 4)) ∪ {3} ∪ ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4))) = 2 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  have e2 : blockWeight (({2} : Finset (Fin 4)) ∪ ({3} : Finset (Fin 4))) = 1 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  have e3 : blockWeight (({2} : Finset (Fin 4)) ∪ ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4))) = 1 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  have e4 : blockWeight (({3} : Finset (Fin 4)) ∪ ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4))) = 1 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  have e5 : blockWeight ({2} : Finset (Fin 4)) = 0 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  have e6 : blockWeight ({3} : Finset (Fin 4)) = 0 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  have e7 : blockWeight ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4)) = 1 := by
    rw [blockWeight_eq]; simp <;> try norm_num
  unfold sorkinI3
  simp only []
  rw [e1, e2, e3, e4, e5, e6, e7]
  norm_num

/--
The escape. The pointwise square of a pairwise generated weight need not be
pairwise generated: the block weight is, its square is not.
-/
theorem block_weight_square_is_not_two_decomposable :
    ¬ TwoDecomposable (fun S => blockWeight S * blockWeight S) := by
  intro h
  have h0 := twoDecomposable_sorkinI3 (fun S => blockWeight S * blockWeight S) h
    {2} {3} ({0, 1} : Finset (Fin 4)) (by decide) (by decide) (by decide)
  rw [block_weight_square_i3] at h0
  exact absurd h0 (by norm_num)

/--
Products add grade: grade one times grade one is grade two (the
Guo-Cao-Chen-Yin corollary in `SorkinGradeIffPairwiseGeneration`), while by
the theorem above the square of a pairwise generated weight is not even
second order. So no composition rule for readouts can be weight
multiplication if the composite must stay second order.
-/

inductive CompositionFinding where
  | vanishingThirdOrderIsKernelGenerationOnThreeAlternatives
  | theArityConverseIsClosedInTheKernelLanguage
  | theBlockWeightIsPairwiseGenerated
  | itsSquareHasThirdOrderInterferenceTwo
  | weightMultiplicationCannotBeTheCompositeReadoutRule
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

def compositionFindings : List CompositionFinding :=
  [ .vanishingThirdOrderIsKernelGenerationOnThreeAlternatives,
    .theArityConverseIsClosedInTheKernelLanguage,
    .theBlockWeightIsPairwiseGenerated,
    .itsSquareHasThirdOrderInterferenceTwo,
    .weightMultiplicationCannotBeTheCompositeReadoutRule ]

theorem composition_records_five_findings :
    compositionFindings.length = 5 := by
  rfl

inductive CompositionOpenObligation where
  | stateCompositionClosureAsAGluingPropertyWithoutPropFlagBags
  | runTheTwoContextTernaryComposabilityProbe
  | replicateDensityCubeParameterCountsExactly
  | gradeTheNoSignallingPolytopeByKernelProducts
  | connectToTwoSegalDecompositionSpaces
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

def compositionOpenObligations : List CompositionOpenObligation :=
  [ .stateCompositionClosureAsAGluingPropertyWithoutPropFlagBags,
    .runTheTwoContextTernaryComposabilityProbe,
    .replicateDensityCubeParameterCountsExactly,
    .gradeTheNoSignallingPolytopeByKernelProducts,
    .connectToTwoSegalDecompositionSpaces ]

theorem composition_keeps_five_open_obligations :
    compositionOpenObligations.length = 5 := by
  rfl

def compositionClaimBoundary : String :=
  "Over three alternatives in Q: a weight has vanishing Sorkin third-order term on all disjoint nonempty triples if and only if it is generated by a kernel on pairs in the kernelWeight sense, so the converse obligation of ArityBoundsInterferenceOrder is discharged. Over four alternatives: the block weight is pairwise generated while its square has third-order interference 2, so the pointwise product of two pairwise generated weights need not be pairwise generated; weight multiplication cannot be the composite readout rule. The composition-forces-binary-arity hypothesis (C-arity) is stated in prose with literature anchors, NOT as a theorem or as abstract Prop flags; the density-cube and purification numbers cited are external results, not results of this repository. Nothing here derives complex amplitudes, a Hilbert carrier, or the Born rule."

